<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Zhou (Wu Sangui)</h1><p class="st-meta">1678 CE – 1681 CE · capital Hengzhou</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The dynasty proclaimed by Wu Sangui, the general who let the Manchus into China and later rebelled against them, during the Revolt of the Three Feudatories.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a>, the Ming commander who opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanhai-pass">Shanhai Pass</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> in 1644, conquered south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, executed the last <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a> emperor in 1662 and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a> almost independently as a feudatory prince. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> moved to abolish the feudatories in 1673, Wu rebelled, declaring a new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wu-zhou">Zhou dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geng-jingzhong">Geng Jingzhong</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shang-zhixin">Shang Zhixin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, Wu at first controlled most of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, but his claim to restore Chinese rule was undermined by his own past. In 1678 he formally proclaimed himself emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hengyang">Hengzhou</a> (Hengyang) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>. His regime was a military state financed by the resources of the south-west.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a> died a few months after his enthronement in 1678. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-shifan">Wu Shifan</a> retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a> as Qing armies advanced, and when the city fell in 1681 he killed himself, ending the revolt and opening the Qing consolidation of the south.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zhou-wu-sangui.jpg" alt="Zhou (Wu Sangui)" width="283" height="375"><figcaption>Image: Author in Qing Dynasty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wu_Sangui.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1678 CE – 1681 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hengzhou</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wu Sangui, Wu Shifan</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mandarin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Zhou (1678-1681)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1680&amp;focus=zhou-wu-sangui" aria-label="Zhou (Wu Sangui) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7602 1340 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zhou (Wu Sangui) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7922 1596L7567 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7922 1596h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7922 1596h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hengzhou. <a href="/atlas?year=1680&amp;focus=zhou-wu-sangui">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
